 ### Volumetric Appearance Architectures
-This variant models RGB and transparency using two identical neural network architectures (128-128-64 MLPs) with GELU activations [S2]. Inputs include trainable positional encoding of the 3D point, spherical harmonics encoding (degree 3) of the view direction, the SDF normal vector, and a geometric feature vector $z$ predicted by k-SDF [S2]. Normals are computed as normalized gradients of the SDFs using finite differences with $\epsilon = 10^{-4}$ [S2].
+Volumetric Appearance Architectures model RGB and transparency using two identical neural network architectures (one for RGB, one for transparency) that encode 3D points with trainable positional encoding and spherical harmonics for view direction [S2]. They differ from the main method by explicitly modeling transparency alongside RGB and using geometric features from k-SDF [S2].
+
+### BRDF Parameterization with Simplified Disney Model
+This variant assigns BRDF properties (albedo $b \in [0, 1]^3$, roughness $r \in [0, 1]$) to each 3D Gaussian using a simplified Disney BRDF model to enable relighting [S3]. The specular term is calculated as $f_{s}(\boldsymbol {\omega }_{o}, \boldsymbol {\omega }_{i})=\frac {D(\boldsymbol {h};r) \cdot F(\boldsymbol {\omega }_{o},\boldsymbol {h}) \cdot G(\boldsymbol {\omega }_{i},\boldsymbol {\omega }_{o}, h; r)}{(\boldsymbol {n} \cdot \boldsymbol {\omega }_{i}) \cdot (\boldsymbol {n} \cdot \boldsymbol {\omega }_{o})}$, where $h = (\omega_i + \omega_o)/2$ [S3]. It differs from the main method by using discrete Gaussian attributes rather than continuous neural fields [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-Time and memory complexity are determined by the number of MLPs (three distinct networks: color, normal, BRDF) and the length of the appearance feature vector $L$ [S1, unofficial]. The method requires storing and decoding three separate outputs per point, increasing computational load compared to single-output color decoders [S1, unofficial]. Specific performance metrics are not given in the sources.
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+The complexity depends on the MLP depth and width. Since three separate MLPs are used ($C_{fuc}$, $n_{fuc}$, $B_{dec}$), the computational cost is roughly triple that of a single-color MLP decoder [S1, unofficial]. Memory usage is higher due to the parameters of three distinct networks [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+Specific performance metrics (PSNR, SSIM, FPS) for this exact configuration are not provided in the sources [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Scaling:**
+Scaling to larger scenes requires efficient feature storage (e.g., voxel grids or hash tables) to retrieve $a(x)$, as the MLPs themselves are point-wise [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** Explicit prediction of normals and BRDF parameters enables physically plausible relighting and normal estimation [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires more parameters than single-output decoders [S1, unofficial]. The separation of features into appearance and other components is not explicitly defined, which may lead to information leakage or insufficient feature capacity [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use:**
+Use when explicit material properties and normals are required for relighting or normal estimation tasks [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+Avoid when computational efficiency is critical and only color output is needed, or when explicit BRDF parameterization is not required [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originating from the STensoRRF paper, this method was proposed to accurately relight radiation fields by approximating incident light fields, including direct and indirect illumination [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitation of standard NeRF by providing explicit geometric and material properties alongside color [S1, unofficial].
+The method originates from the STensoRRF paper, which proposes a tensorial relighting radiance field for single-tensor scene representation [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the need for accurate relighting by approximating incident light fields, including direct and indirect lighting [S1, unofficial]. The approach imitates incident light simulation methods using spherical Gaussians for global illumination [S1, unofficial].
 
